ory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Strengths: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence (weather, computers). Weakness: interpersonal (nasty remarks) harms crew cooperation, memory for numbers could affect attention/accuracy. So mixed.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Negatives: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Weaknesses: crew cooperation, adaptability, learning.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Negatives: regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, attention, problem solving, responsibility. Weakness: crew cooperation (know-it-all, hot temper) could affect teamwork.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some interpersonal issues. Which candidate has least severe? Let's weigh.\n\nCandidate B: nasty remarks is serious for crew cooperation. Candidate A: show-off, not open to new ideas - also problematic. Candidate C: egocentric, communication difficulty, not willing to further education - also problematic. Candidate D: hot temper, know-it-all - also problematic.\n\nWhich has strongest technical and stress? All have good. Candidate B handles stress well, B good weather assessment. Candidate C calm in crisis, quick decisions. Candidate D responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates, solves problems. Candidate A anticipates dangerous situations.\n\nWhich has best reliability? B very conscientious. A leadership but maybe not reliability. D takes responsibility seriously. So B and D strong.\n\nInterpersonal: B nasty remarks - severe. D hot temper - also severe. A show-off - maybe less severe? Not open to new ideas - moderate. C egocentric, communication difficulty - also severe.\n\nThus maybe A is best overall despite attitude, because technical and leadership strong, and attitude issues less directly harmful than nasty remarks or hot temper? Show-off could affect crew but maybe manageable.\n\nWe need to argue with evidence. Since no prior discussion, we can state recommendation for Candidate A, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns, but argue they are outweighed.\n\nLet's craft public message: \"I recommend Candidate A\". Provide facts: anticipate dangerous situations, excellent spatial vision, leadership. Also note show-off and not open to new ideas as concerns but argue that technical competence and reliability are critical for long-distance flights, and these concerns can be mitigated with training.\n\nOk."}
