 list: \"C can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education, puts concern for others above everything, has excellent attention skills.\" No mention of technical competence. So C lacks explicit technical competence, but decision quality and attention are strong.\n\nB has technical competence (weather assessment, computer skills). Reliability (very conscientious). Stress resilience (handles stress well). Crew cooperation issues (pretentious, wrong tone). So trade-off.\n\nWe could argue that C's crew cooperation and reliability are critical, and his decision making and attention are strong, while his communication difficulty and egocentric may be mitigated. However lack of technical competence may be a gap.\n\nD has problem solving, concentration, responsibility, but moody and loner.\n\nGiven the role is long-distance pilot, technical competence and stress handling are crucial. B seems strong there. C's lack of technical competence may be a bigger issue.\n\nThus likely B remains best. As Agent 1, we can now align with B, acknowledging earlier points, and perhaps bring up that while C's reliability is excellent, his lack of technical competence and communication issues make him less suitable than B.\n\nThus we will vote for Candidate B.\n\nWe need to produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation B, refer to prior discussion (Agent 2's shift, Agent 3's recommendation), use explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills; also mention B's pretentiousness and wrong tone but argue less severe. Compare with C's reliability and crew cooperation but lack of technical competence. Conclude B is best.\n\nMETADATA_JSON: {\"agent\":\"agent_1\",\"vote\":\"Candidate B\"}\n\nProceed."}
